---
import DocsShell from '../../components/DocsShell.astro'
import Code from '../../components/Code.astro'

const crSetup = `
ownci setup --repo owner/name --mode container-runner
ownci start --detach
`

const crWorkflow = `
jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: [ownci]           # no container: needed
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: sudo apt-get install -y jq && make test
`

const customImage = `
docker build -t my/runner:22.04 -f e2e/images/ubuntu22.Dockerfile .   # in a clone of the OwnCI repository
ownci setup --repo owner/name --mode container-runner --image my/runner:22.04
`

const teamSetup = `
ownci setup --repo owner/name --labels dev-<your-github-username>   # each developer, once
`

const teamWorkflow = `
jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: [ownci, "dev-\${{ github.actor }}"]   # the user who opened or pushed to the PR
    container: node:22
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: npm ci && npm run build
`

const maintainerWorkflow = `
# .github/workflows/ci.yml, on main
on:
  pull_request_target:
    branches: [main]
permissions:
  contents: read             # pull_request_target can otherwise get a write token
jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: [ownci, "dev-\${{ github.event.pull_request.user.login }}"]   # the PR author's machine
    container: node:22
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          ref: \${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}   # the PR's code, not main's
          persist-credentials: false
      - run: npm ci && npm run build
`

const severalRepos = `
ownci --data-dir ~/.local/share/ownci-app setup --repo owner/app --name ownci-mybox-app
ownci --data-dir ~/.local/share/ownci-app start --detach
ownci --data-dir ~/.local/share/ownci-lib setup --repo owner/lib --name ownci-mybox-lib
ownci --data-dir ~/.local/share/ownci-lib start --detach
`
---

<DocsShell
  title="Workflows"
  description="Run workflows without a container: line, share one workflow file across a team, let only maintainers decide where jobs run, and serve several repositories from one machine."
>
  <section class="docs-section" id="container-runner">
    <p class="section-kicker">Container-runner mode</p>
    <h2>Run every job in a container, no <code>container:</code> needed</h2>
    <p>
      Workflows written for GitHub-hosted runners usually have no <code>container:</code> line. In
      container-runner mode they run unchanged. To switch an existing runner: <code>ownci stop</code>,
      then <code>ownci setup --force --mode container-runner</code>.
    </p>
    <Code label="shell" code={crSetup} />
    <Code label=".github/workflows/ci.yml" code={crWorkflow} />
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Jobs without <code>container:</code></strong> run inside the runner container, as your user ID, with passwordless <code>sudo</code> and the Docker CLI.</li>
      <li><strong>Jobs with <code>container:</code></strong> behave exactly as in host mode: the job container is a sibling on your Docker daemon.</li>
      <li><strong>Every job gets a fresh container.</strong> Anything a job changes outside the workspace (<code>apt-get install</code>, <code>/tmp</code>, <code>$HOME</code>) is gone for the next one, and the workspace is wiped as in host mode. Expect a few seconds between jobs while the new container connects.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>The runner image</h3>
    <p>
      The default is the official <code>ghcr.io/actions/actions-runner</code> (Ubuntu 24.04), pinned by
      digest: about 2 GB, pulled once at setup. It has git, curl and the Docker CLI, but not the
      hosted runners’ preinstalled toolchains; <code>actions/setup-*</code> actions install what you need.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>--image</code> picks another one, at <code>setup</code> or for one session at <code>start</code>.
      It <strong>must</strong> contain <code>bash</code>, <code>setpriv</code>, <code>useradd</code>/<code>groupadd</code>/<code>usermod</code>, <code>getent</code>, <code>stat</code>, the <code>docker</code> CLI and ICU, and <strong>should</strong> contain <code>git</code> and <code>sudo</code>. <code>setup</code> refuses an image that lacks a required tool.
    </p>
    <Code label="shell" code={customImage} />
    <p class="callout">
      The runner directory must be mountable at its own path: a plain Linux path without symlinks,
      not under <code>/mnt/c</code>. The default data directory is fine.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="team">
    <p class="section-kicker">Teams</p>
    <h2>One workflow file for a whole team</h2>
    <p>
      Give each developer’s runner their own label (<code>dev-</code> plus their GitHub username) and
      let the workflow pick the runner of whoever triggered it:
    </p>
    <Code label="shell" code={teamSetup} />
    <Code label=".github/workflows/ci.yml" code={teamWorkflow} />
    <ul>
      <li>If a developer’s runner is offline, their job waits in the queue. It never lands on someone else’s runner.</li>
      <li>Bots such as Dependabot match no runner.</li>
      <li>Each developer needs admin access to the repository to run <code>ownci setup</code>.</li>
      <li>Use <code>github.event.pull_request.user.login</code> instead of <code>github.actor</code> to target the PR <em>author</em> rather than whoever pushed last.</li>
      <li>Keep the <code>dev-</code> prefix. Labels are case-insensitive, and without a prefix a username can equal another runner label: a real GitHub account called <code>ownci</code> exists, and its jobs would become <code>runs-on: [ownci, ownci]</code>, which every OwnCI runner on the repository matches.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout">
      The label routes jobs; it does not restrict who can reach a runner. Anyone who can change the
      workflow can point <code>runs-on</code> at another developer’s label. To let only maintainers
      decide, see <a href="#maintainer-control">the next section</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="maintainer-control">
    <p class="section-kicker">Maintainer control</p>
    <h2>Let only maintainers decide where jobs run</h2>
    <p>
      With <code>on: pull_request</code>, GitHub runs the workflow file from the PR, so a PR can
      change <code>runs-on</code> to any developer’s label. And anyone with write access can push a
      branch with a workflow of its own (<code>on: push</code>) that targets any runner on the
      repository. If maintainers should have full control over which jobs reach which machine:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Run CI from <code>main</code>’s workflow file with <code>pull_request_target</code>.</strong>
        It always uses the workflow from the base branch, so a PR cannot change <code>runs-on</code>;
        it still tests the PR’s code.
        <Code label=".github/workflows/ci.yml" code={maintainerWorkflow} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Protect the workflow files on <code>main</code>.</strong> Require pull request
        reviews on <code>main</code> (branch protection or a branch ruleset) with <em>Require review
        from Code Owners</em>, and a <code>CODEOWNERS</code> entry such as
        <code>/.github/workflows/ @owner/maintainers</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Block workflow changes on every other branch.</strong> A push ruleset with
        <em>Restrict file paths</em> set to <code>.github/workflows/**</code>, with maintainers on the
        bypass list, stops anyone from pushing a branch whose own workflow targets a runner. Push
        rulesets need GitHub Team or Enterprise and a private or internal repository. Without this
        step, anyone with write access can still reach every runner on the repository.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Don’t use secrets or caches in this workflow.</strong> The PR’s code runs in the same job, so anything the job can read, the PR can read, and a cache it saves is visible to <code>main</code>’s workflows.</li>
      <li><strong><code>pull_request_target</code> ignores the fork pull request approval setting:</strong> it always runs. Keep the author’s <code>dev-</code> label in <code>runs-on</code>; a contributor without a runner of their own gets a job that waits in the queue and reaches no one’s machine. Never combine this trigger with <code>runs-on: [ownci]</code> alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Maintainers decide where a job runs, not what it runs.</strong> The PR’s scripts (<code>npm ci</code>, its tests) still run on the author’s machine. Anyone with write access who pushes to someone else’s PR branch runs their code on that author’s machine.</li>
      <li>A PR that changes CI is tested with <code>main</code>’s workflow; the change applies once it is merged, and then reaches every developer’s machine. Review workflow changes like code.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="several-repositories">
    <p class="section-kicker">Several repositories</p>
    <h2>One data directory per repository</h2>
    <p>
      One data directory holds one runner for one repository. For a second repository, give it its
      own data directory and runner name:
    </p>
    <Code label="shell" code={severalRepos} />
    <ul>
      <li>Every later command (<code>status</code>, <code>logs</code>, <code>stop</code>, <code>uninstall</code>) needs the same <code>--data-dir</code>, or <code>export OWNCI_DATA_DIR=…</code> in that shell.</li>
      <li>In container-runner mode the names must be distinct, because the runner container is called <code>ownci-&lt;runner name&gt;</code> and Docker container names are global.</li>
      <li>The runners are independent: each runs one job at a time, and they share the machine’s CPU, memory and Docker daemon.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</DocsShell>
